  "operative_text": "(a_in_person_id) not stated.\n(b_no_id_remedy)\n(section) 7-15-385(E) (absentee return only; no provisional-ballot, cure, affidavit or free-ID remedy is stated)\n(quote) An election official must not accept a return-addressed envelope until the provisions of this section have been met.\n(c_mail_ballot_id)\n(section) 7-15-385(D)(1) (photo ID shown when the sealed absentee envelope is delivered in person)\n(quote) When an applicant, or an applicant's authorized immediate family member or authorized representative, presents himself to deliver a return-addressed envelope pursuant to this section, he must produce a valid and current: (a) driver's license issued by a state within the United States; (b) another form of identification containing a photograph issued by the Department of Motor Vehicles or its equivalent by a state within the United States; (c) passport; (d) military identification containing a photograph issued by the federal government; or (e) South Carolina voter registration card containing a photograph of the voter.\n(section) 7-15-385(D)(2)\n(quote) An election official must verify that the name and photograph on the identification is the applicant, or the applicant's authorized immediate family member or authorized representative, as applicable.\n(section) 7-15-330(B)(2) (identifying number, not a photo ID, to request the absentee application)\n(quote) A person who makes a request for an application to vote by absentee ballot, either for himself or on behalf of another elector as permitted by this section, must provide the following: (a) for the elector for whom the request is being made, the elector's: (i) name; (ii) date of birth; and (iii) last four digits of his social security number;\n(d_proof_of_citizenship)\n(section) 7-3-70(c), added by SECTION 32 (database report; no voter is asked for proof of citizenship)\n(quote) The Department of Motor Vehicles must furnish the executive director a monthly report of all non-United States citizens who are issued a driver's license or identification card. All reports must contain the name of the driver or identification cardholder, social security number, if any, and date of birth.\n(section) 7-5-340(A)(1) lead-in\n(quote) ensure that the name of a qualified elector is removed from the official list of eligible voters within seven days of receipt of information confirming:\n(section) 7-5-340(A)(1)(d), added\n(quote) the elector is not a citizen of the United States;\n(e_other_rule)\n(section) 7-5-170(2) (added to the registration oath)\n(quote) and that, to my knowledge, I am neither registered nor intend to register to vote in another state or county\n(section) 7-5-186(A)\n(quote) The executive director must conduct an annual general registration list maintenance program to maintain accurate voter registration records in the statewide voter registration system.\n(section) 7-15-430(B) (provisional ballot for a voter issued an absentee ballot who has not returned it)\n(quote) The provisional ballot must only be counted if the absentee ballot is not received by the time for the closing of the polls on election day\n(other) Original bill (2020-2021 text): 48-22-40 requires the State Geological Survey Unit to map the state with LiDAR (laser elevation data) by a set date and at least every seven years, sharing results with the Department of Natural Resources flood program. No election content.\n(other) House amendment (2022-05-03) keeps the LiDAR section as SECTION 1 (date moved to December 31, 2022) and adds the election text of H 4919 as SECTION 2 onward; the Senate-amended version (2022-05-11) rewrites the election text as SECTIONS 1 to 45 and the LiDAR section no longer appears in the saved 05-11 text (the title still names it).\n(other) SECTION 1: 7-13-25 early voting at 1 to 7 county early voting centers for the two weeks before every election (no ID rule attached).\n(other) SECTIONS 2 to 4, 6: candidate filing limits; absentee witness must be 18 and print his name; new absentee reasons list.\n(other) SECTION 5: 7-15-330 who may request an absentee application, five-request limit, requestor data including last four social security digits, applications due eleventh day before the election.\n(other) SECTION 7: 7-15-385 return rules, five-envelope limit, photo ID shown on delivery; SECTION 8 and 33: absentee envelopes examined from two days before election day and counted from 7:00 a.m. on election day; SECTION 9: provisional ballot for absentee voters who have not returned the ballot.\n(other) SECTIONS 10 to 21: registration oath addition, ballot authentication, voting-system procurement and security rules, reports of deaths and incapacity, annual list maintenance, faster removals including non-citizens (7-5-340).\n(other) SECTIONS 22 to 25, 38: State Law Enforcement Division hotline, annual voter-roll report, Senate and House standing to intervene in election-law cases, audit methods, penalty increases, ban on paying for absentee ballots.\n(other) SECTIONS 27 to 31, 34: State Election Commission composition, appointment and removal; executive director appointed with advice and consent of the Senate; standardized county processes; ban on private funding; security standards.\n(other) SECTION 32: DMV monthly report of non-citizen licence and ID holders to the State Election Commission; SECTION 35: voter education program; SECTION 36, 37: authorized-representative definition and no absentee application unless law allows; SECTION 39: campaign material ban widened to 500 feet and the early voting period; SECTION 41: 7-15-470 repealed (ends in-person absentee voting).\n(other) SECTION 40 and 45: transition dates (forms by May 31, 2022; absentee requests honoured under old law until 5:00 p.m. on May 31, 2022); SECTION 2 effective January 1, 2023, SECTIONS 3 and 6 and the witness-name check July 1, 2022, all others on approval by the Governor.\n(other) version: 2020-12-09_Introduced: no ID or citizenship text in slots a-d; a one-section bill on LiDAR mapping by the State Geological Survey Unit.\n(other) version: 2021-03-02_Comm-Sub (Senate committee): still LiDAR only; no ID or citizenship text.\n(other) version: 2022-04-27_Comm-Sub (House committee): still LiDAR only (date moved to December 31, 2022); no ID or citizenship text.\n(other) version: 2022-05-03_Amended (House floor, as passed by the House 5/3-5/4): LiDAR section plus the House-passed election text of H 4919. Slot c: last four social security digits on the absentee application (7-15-340(B)(2)) and photo ID for an authorized returnee delivering in person (7-15-385(B), five forms including 'South Carolina driver's license' and 'South Carolina voter registration card containing a photograph of the voter pursuant to Section 7-5-675'). No slot a, b or d text; no citizenship text.\n(other) version: 2022-05-11_Amended (Senate amended the House amendment, adopted 5/11; House concurred 5/12; ratified R 165): rewrites to the Senate-passed text of H 4919. Slot c now also covers the applicant delivering in person, with 'state' licences and 'DMV or its equivalent by a state within the United States'; the 7-15-340 section is dropped and 7-15-330(B)(2) takes name, date of birth and last four social security digits; adds slot b-type refusal to accept the envelope (7-15-385(E)); adds slot d database text (7-3-70(c) DMV non-citizen report; 7-5-340(A)(1)(d) removal of non-citizens). Quotes are from this version.\n(other) version: 2022-05-23_Amended: the saved file is an error page and has no bill text; superseded by zz-final_ratified.\n(other) version: zz-final_ratified (ratified 12 May 2022, Act 150, no markers): every ID, absentee-application, citizenship and other quoted rule in slots b-e matches the 2022-05-11_Amended wording word for word. Slot a is still not stated (no 7-13-710 text). The ratified text has no LiDAR section.\n(other) note: S 108 became Act 150 of 2022 (ratified R 165 on 5/12/2022, signed 5/13). It was a one-section LiDAR mapping bill in the Senate (2021), was used by the House as a vehicle for the H 4919 election text, and the Senate rewrote that text on 5/11; the House concurred 5/12. The 2021 Senate vote (42-0) was on the LiDAR text only. Quotes now come from the ratified text (zz-final_ratified); all were checked and found there, none missing. The ratified text has no LiDAR section and does not mention 7-13-710, so in-person photo ID is 'not stated'. The Senate rewrite matches H 4919 as passed by the Senate on all ID, absentee-application and citizenship sections. The 05-23 file is an error page. Quotes join list items into one line.",
